artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a cell-free massive multiple-input multiple-output (CF-MMIMO) wireless network in which a large number of geographically distributed access points (APs), each equipped with multiple antennas, are connected via fronthaul links to one or more central processing units and jointly serve all user devices in the coverage area without forming traditional cells. Assume the network must support massive machine-type communication (mMTC) traffic with the following characteristics:

(i) a very large population of low-complexity, energy-limited devices, of which only an unknown sporadic subset is active in any given coherence block;
(ii) each active device transmits a very short packet (on the order of tens to hundreds of bits) under strict latency and ultra-reliability constraints;
(iii) the channel coherence block contains too few symbols to assign mutually orthogonal pilot sequences to all potential devices, so non-orthogonal pilots and pilot collisions are unavoidable;
(iv) the activity pattern and traffic statistics of the devices are not known a priori at the network;
(v) acquiring accurate channel state information (CSI) for all potential devices at all APs is infeasible or prohibitively costly, and standard stochastic channel models may not faithfully capture the actual propagation conditions.

Design and analyze a complete framework for supporting mMTC in such a CF-MMIMO system, comprising:

(a) a random-access protocol (preamble/pilot design and contention resolution) that operates without per-device orthogonal pilots and provides provable guarantees on activity-detection error probability as a function of the number of devices, number of APs, number of antennas per AP, pilot length, and signal-to-noise ratio;
(b) joint device activity detection, channel estimation, and data decoding algorithms that exploit the distributed AP architecture, are robust to model mismatch in the channel statistics, and have computational and fronthaul-signaling complexity scaling favorably with the number of APs and devices;
(c) a control-plane and data-transmission scheme (including possible grant-free or one-shot/unsourced random access) whose total signaling overhead per delivered information bit remains bounded as the device population grows;
(d) information-theoretic and finite-blocklength performance bounds quantifying the achievable trade-offs between latency, reliability, spectral efficiency, energy per bit, and the amount of CSI used.

Determine whether such a framework exists that simultaneously satisfies the latency, reliability, scalability, and CSI-cost requirements above, and provide matching achievability and converse results.

